used by best before  if you've ever been confused by all the  different language around expiry dates  on food you're not alone  eu citizens waste 88 million tons of  food every year  up to 10 of that is because date  labeling is misunderstood  logically then if more of us understood  the date marking we would throw away  less food every year  in fact up to 8 million tons less  when you buy a food product at a  supermarket you're going to see a range  of mandatory information on the  packaging nutritional information  ingredients and a bunch of different  dates  used by and best before are the most  common ones  so what do they mean  let's break it down starting with you  spy  in a 2015 study the european union asked  europeans if they knew what u-spy stands  for more than half got it wrong  the job of the use by label is to tell  you until when a food product can be  eaten without any risk to your health  it is mandatory for perishable foods  such as meat fish and some types of  cheese  so if you see this it means you can eat  the food until the end of this date but  not after  even if you think it looks and smells  fine  now best before tells you about the  quality of the food product  it means that it's perfectly fine to eat  a product after this date but it may no  longer be at its best quality and could  have lost some of its optimal flavor and  texture so if you see this it means you  are to judge for yourself if you want to  eat the food depending on its appearance  and smell  so to recap use pie is about food safety  best before has to do with food quality  those are the basics  now there are a few notes  not all the food we eat requires day  marking  uncut fresh fruit and vegetables for  instance  here no use buy or best before dates are  required  also date marking only works when  storage instructions for a food product  are respected and if the packaging isn't  damaged  a use by date on a raw stake for example  won't be any good if it's been left  outside on a warm day  on the other hand you can extend the  shelf 5 of a product beyond the use by  date by freezing it right after you buy  it  you do have to make sure it's frozen at  the right temperature packaged well kept  away from any contaminants and that it  has not been frozen before  finally you absolutely have to make sure  you follow instructions on consumption  such as consume within two days of  opening  households are responsible for more than  half of europe's food waste so a big  chunk of the responsibility is not up to  supermarkets or governments or  restaurants  it is up to us  we have a big power departures power to  choose what  we  buy and of course how we use  store  refrigerate cook  the food items  european union regulators understand  that date labeling on food products  isn't always easy to understand  so they're planning on reviewing the  rules by the end of 2022 the confusion  with date marking is sometimes linked to  translation in some languages the  difference between use by and best  before  seems not be clear enough  so what is missing  i would say all over the world not only  in europe we need to invest more on food  education basics  and then everything will be more clear  and  waste will be reduced  for now though the best we can do is  learn to read labels properly  by doing that we would not only help  reduce food waste but also fight climate  change because we'd save natural  resources spend less money at the  supermarket and make healthier food  choices  and there's no expiry date for those  things
 

